  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1244 PM CDT Sat Aug 8 2026\n\nA cluster of strong thunderstorms has persisted into eastern\nNebraska despite less than favorable conditions. These\nthunderstorms are likely being aided by gravity waves as\nevidenced by Hastings radar data and a convectively generated\nMCV. This complex is likely to persist over the next few hours\nas it pushes south across eastern-central Nebraska, southeast\nNebraska, and far southwest Iowa. Severe weather remains a\npossibility with these thunderstorms, but the threat is very low\n(5%). If any thunderstorms were to become severe. Damaging winds\nand hail are the primary hazards. Outside of this, hazy skies\ndue to wildfire smoke aloft and temperatures in the 80s are\nanticipated this afternoon. \n\nHeading into this evening and tonight, any residual outflow from\nthe aforementioned thunderstorm cluster and the nose of a low- \nlevel jet are forecast to act as a focus for additional shower \nand thunderstorm development. It remains uncertain how far \nwestward this development will occur, but there is a low chance \n(15-30%) of thunderstorms over far eastern Nebraska and western \nIowa tonight. The better chance of thunderstorms is primarily \njust east of the area. Once again, severe weather in the form of\ndamaging winds and hail is possible, but the threat is low \n(5%). \n\nA lull in the activity is likely on Sunday as the ridge over the\nGreat Basin builds slightly. This helps push our temperatures\ninto the 90s. Temperatures may even approach 100 across southern\nNebraska, though this remains uncertain. Continued wildfire\nsmoke aloft will limit solar insolation and could prevent\ntemperatures from getting as hot as they otherwise would be able\nto. That said, it will be humid with heat indices around 100. \n\nThe heat does not last as repeated weak troughs rounding the\nridge will slide through the northern Plains and Midwest each\nday throughout the week. Thunderstorm chances are anticipated \nmainly during the overnight time period with the aid of the low-\nlevel jet to help overcome warm low-level temperatures. That \nsaid, confidence in exact timing of thunderstorms is subject to \nchange with mesoscale features potentially helping develop \nadditional thunderstorms during the day. The overall chance of \nthunderstorms is relatively low (20-40%) until later in the week\nwhen chances increase (40-70%). With moderate instability and \nmoderate mid-level winds, severe weather is possible with \nthunderstorms, though this threat remains low (5%). Damaging \nwinds and hail would continue to be the main hazards. Outside of\nthunderstorms, a combination of repeated rain, cloud cover, and\ndecreasing heights decrease temperatures each afternoon. It \nappears temperatures may even fall into the mid 70s by the end \nof next week. \n\nWildfire smoke may persist over the coming week as northwest\nwinds aloft keep smoke in the vicinity. Most of this smoke\nremains aloft with very little reaching the surface. That said,\nhazy skies are likely with at least a minor decrease to air\nquality.",
